{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"getEstimatedWaterAllocation","context":"#### Samples\n\n```\n// GetEstimatedWaterAllocationSuccess\nval resp = sustainabilityClient.getEstimatedWaterAllocation {\n timePeriod = TimePeriod {\n start = \"2025-01-01T00:00:00.00Z\"\n end = \"2026-01-01T00:00:00.00Z\"\n }\n groupBy = listOf<Dimension>(\n Dimension.fromValue(\"SERVICE\")\n )\n allocationTypes = listOf<WaterAllocationType>(\n WaterAllocationType.fromValue(\"TOTAL_WATER_WITHDRAWALS\")\n )\n granularity = TimeGranularity.fromValue(\"YEARLY_CALENDAR\")\n}\n```\n\nContent copied to clipboard\n\nGenerated by\nDokka\n\n© 2026, Amazon Web Services, Inc. or its affiliates. All rights reserved","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdk-for-kotlin/api/latest/sustainability/aws.sdk.kotlin.services.sustainability/get-estimated-water-allocation.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"IAmazonSustainability.GetEstimatedWaterAllocation Method (GetEstimatedWaterAllocationRequest) | AWS SDK for .NET Version 4","context":"## Syntax\n\nC#\n\n```\npublic abstract GetEstimatedWaterAllocationResponse GetEstimatedWaterAllocation(\n GetEstimatedWaterAllocationRequest request\n)\n```\n\n### **Parameters**\n\nrequest\n: Type: Amazon.Sustainability.Model.GetEstimatedWaterAllocationRequest\n\n Container for the necessary parameters to execute the GetEstimatedWaterAllocation service method.\n\n### **Return Value**\n\nType: GetEstimatedWaterAllocationResponse\n\nThe response from the GetEstimatedWaterAllocation service method, as returned by Sustainability","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkfornet/v4/apidocs/items/Sustainability/MISustainabilityGetEstimatedWaterAllocationGetEstimatedWaterAllocationRequest.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Integrate AWS carbon and water data into your ESG reporting | AWS Global Infrastructure and Sustainability Blog","context":"## 2. Native connectors in 3rd party tools\n\nYour ESG platform may offer a pre-built AWS connector that automatically imports AWS carbon and water data. The concrete steps depend on the tool you use. Typically, you can follow these steps:\n\n1. Provide access, typically using one of the following:\n 1. an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) user\n 2. an IAM role that trusts the tool’s AWS account\n2. Grant least privileges, depending on the API used to access the data:\n 1. The IAM policy actions for the AWS Sustainability API are:\n\n```\n \"Action\": [\n \"sustainability:GetEstimatedCarbonEmissions\",\n \"sustainability:GetEstimatedCarbonEmissionsDimensionValues\",\n \"sustainability:GetEstimatedWaterAllocation\",\n \"sustainability:GetEstimatedWaterAllocationDimensionValues\"\n ]\n```\n\nb. To read Carbon emissions Data Exports on Amazon S3, the 3rd party tool requires at least read access to the objects in S3.\n\n 3. Configure the ESG reporting tool to automatically pull carbon and water data on a scheduled basis for dashboards, reports, and analytics.\n\nAn example of a native connector is the **Sweep sustainability intelligence platform**. It provides a native AWS connector that you can authorize and enable. Once configured, carbon emissions data flows automatically into Sweep, consolidated alongside other emission sources.\n\nIf your ESG reporting tool has a native connector for the AWS Sustainability API or Carbon Emissions Data Exports, that is the fastest path to automation. If it does not, or if you need more control over the data delivery, the following section walks you through building a custom solution","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/infrastructure-sustainability/integrate-aws-carbon-and-water-data-into-your-esg-reporting/"}]}}

