{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS Sustainability service now includes water withdrawals data - AWS","context":"AWS Sustainability, a service offered by Amazon Web Services (AWS), has expanded its capabilities to include annual water withdrawals data for workloads. This enhancement enables organizations to obtain a comprehensive understanding of their environmental impact, as it complements the existing carbon emissions data. The water withdrawals data is accessible through the AWS Sustainability console and API, and is available on an annual basis by AWS Region, service, and AWS account. The data represents the total volume of water withdrawn for data center operations, with efficiency improvements reflected as lower withdrawal volumes. The water withdrawals data is provided at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where the service is available. To get started, visit the AWS Sustainability user guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/aws-sustainability-water-withdrawals/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"waf","skill_description":"Configures AWS WAF to filter web traffic: creating web access control lists (web ACLs) on CloudFront, Application Load Balancers, API Gateway, and AppSync; AWS Managed Rules tuned in Count mode; rate-based rules for HTTP floods; IP set and geographic match rules; Bot Control (Common and Targeted); turning bot labels into a confidence signal; stripping spoofed inbound x-amzn-waf-* headers; recovering the real client IP behind a CDN; Fraud Control (account takeover and account creation fraud prevention); and logging and request sampling. Use when the user wants to protect a web application or API from common exploits, bots, credential stuffing, fake-account creation, or HTTP floods at the application layer (layer 7). Routes to the right per-task procedure in references. Do NOT use for L3/L4 DDoS protection (shieldadvanced skill), multi-account WAF rollout (firewallmanager skill), CloudFront configuration (cloudfront skill), or Route 53 health checks or records (route53 skill).\n\n\nServices: waf, wafv2, cloudfront, elb, apigateway, appsync\nTasks: configure, secure, debug\nPersona: developer, devops, security-engineer\nWorkload: security, web-application\n*","skill_name":"waf"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS launches Sustainability console for carbon emissions tracking - AWS","context":"AWS has introduced the AWS Sustainability console, a new, standalone service that enables sustainability professionals to access carbon emissions data from their AWS usage without requiring billing permissions. This service builds upon the features of the Customer Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) in the AWS Billing console","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/aws-launches-sustainability-console/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"managing-data-asset-access","skill_description":"Grants, requests, audits and removes access to data assets in an AWS data catalog (the Amazon SageMaker Catalog supported by Amazon DataZone) through catalog subscriptions over published listings. Covers requesting a subscription, approving or rejecting requests with a recorded decision, sharing a listing with a project, revoking one or many subscriptions, and confirming grant settlement. Triggers on: give another team access to a table, request access to a dataset, approve or reject an access request, share a listing with a project, who has access to this table, revoke access, bulk revoke, is the access actually gone, subscription grant status. Not for: IAM roles, policies or STS (use aws-iam), S3 bucket policies (use securing-s3-buckets), Clean Rooms permissions (use aws-cleanrooms), or writing catalog metadata, glossaries and forms, publishing listings, or retiring or deleting an asset that other teams are subscribed to and the order of operations that needs (use managing-data-catalog)\n\n\nServices: datazone\nTasks: deploy, audit\nPersona: data-engineer, developer, architect\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"managing-data-asset-access"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-marketplace-metering","skill_description":"Deploys, queries, and debugs AWS Marketplace usage-based (PAYG) metering — the pipeline (ResolveCustomer, BatchMeterUsage, EventBridge via SAM) and querying/debugging metering records, statuses, failures, discrepancies. Covers Concurrent Agreements (CustomerAWSAccountId + LicenseArn), multi-product, CloudTrail Event History (90 days; never CloudTrail Lake), Seller Reports (Billed Revenue/Usage columns + CSV/SDDS parsing), existing-stack detection, metering region for BatchMeterUsage/MeterUsage (DisabledApiException), metered AMI/Container + IAM roles, where registration data lives (customer-profile table), legacy ProductCode + CustomerIdentifier, DuplicateRecord/CustomerNotSubscribed, bad LicenseArn. Also SCOPES adjacent asks — flat-rate/SaaS-Subscription \"do I still meter\", CCP included-units + overage, contract term/annual renewal, forecasting future bills/usage — with the right answer or a one-line redirect. Use for any BatchMeterUsage/ResolveCustomer or SaaS usage-billing question.\n\nServices: aws-marketplace, marketplace-metering\nTasks: deploy, integrate, query, debug, troubleshoot, analyze\nPersona: developer\nWorkload: saas\n*","skill_name":"aws-marketplace-metering"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Look up AWS docs for the GetEstimatedWaterAllocation API and its IAM permission."}

